+
+## Before proposing another service
+
+For whoever proposes a new service: start with the web app and Postgres that larder already operates. A new service
+must do something Postgres cannot, and two volunteers must be able to restore it. A proposal should explain the
+capability Postgres cannot provide and identify those two volunteers.
+
+This rule comes from the volunteers' limited capacity to run and recover services:
+
+- [Sessions](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) went into Postgres in 2023 because it survived deploys without
+  adding Redis for the volunteers to run.
+- [Background jobs](adr/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) moved to Postgres after Redis evicted 300 receipt
+  jobs in March 2025, when only one volunteer knew how to restore it.
+- In [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), Elasticsearch was proposed for slow, incomplete pantry item search. Testing
+  Postgres full-text search on item names brought the reported search from four seconds to 40 ms and found
+  "Rice, brown" when searching for "rice". The proposer chose that approach instead.
+
+External services are allowed: larder already uses a card gateway, and a hosted service that no volunteer has to
+operate is an option. The rule limits the operational burden volunteers take on; it is not a ban on services.
